From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: psigod <psigod@example.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 11:27:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: v4l2-common: A negative tv_usec counts as negative

FFmpeg's V4L2 mem2mem decoders and encoders (libavcodec v4l2_buffers.c,
v4l2_set_pts()) split a timestamp with C's truncating / and %, so a
negative one that isn't a whole number of seconds gets a negative
tv_usec: -0.966 s becomes tv_sec 0, tv_usec -966037. The u32 cast here
turns that into 4293 s, the decoder hands the frame back with that
timestamp, and FFmpeg can't place it. Negative timestamps are common in
FFmpeg: after an accurate seek, the frames before the seek point are
decoded with negative timestamps and dropped by timestamp, and files
whose timestamps start below zero have them.

Use the low 32 bits of tv_usec as signed. Valid input (0 to 999999) is
unchanged and the upper bits are still ignored for 32-bit user space; an
invalid negative tv_usec now means what FFmpeg meant. The way back is
already right: the timestamp is returned normalized (tv_sec -1, tv_usec
34000 for -0.966 s), which FFmpeg reads correctly.

Tested on an SM-T820 (Venus): ffmpeg -ss 3 with h264_v4l2m2m now gives
the software decoder's frames and timestamps bit for bit (before: frames
stamped 4294 s), a stream shifted by -0.5 s decodes with the right
timestamps, full-file H.264, HEVC, VP9 and VP8 decodes are unchanged
(bit-exact), and GStreamer's v4l2h264dec is unaffected.

Signed-off-by: psigod <psigod@example.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 include/media/v4l2-common.h | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/include/media/v4l2-common.h b/include/media/v4l2-common.h
index edd416178..56eb803f1 100644
--- a/include/media/v4l2-common.h
+++ b/include/media/v4l2-common.h
@@ -715,11 +715,13 @@ static inline u64 v4l2_buffer_get_timestamp(const struct v4l2_buffer *buf)
 {
 	/*
 	 * When the timestamp comes from 32-bit user space, there may be
-	 * uninitialized data in tv_usec, so cast it to u32.
-	 * Otherwise allow invalid input for backwards compatibility.
+	 * uninitialized data in tv_usec, so use its low 32 bits only.
+	 * Otherwise allow invalid input for backwards compatibility: a
+	 * negative tv_usec counts as negative (FFmpeg gives a timestamp of
+	 * -0.5 s as tv_sec 0, tv_usec -500000), instead of as 4294 s.
 	 */
 	return buf->timestamp.tv_sec * NSEC_PER_SEC +
-		(u32)buf->timestamp.tv_usec * NSEC_PER_USEC;
+		(s64)(s32)buf->timestamp.tv_usec * NSEC_PER_USEC;
 }
 
 static inline void v4l2_buffer_set_timestamp(struct v4l2_buffer *buf,
